WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Sampling is one of MCP's most advanced features, and it's worth a detour.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:12.000
It lets a server ask the client to make an LLM inference call and send the results back.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Coming up in this video, we'll cover why this design choice keeps control where it belongs,

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
and how to implement a sampling handler in your own MCP client.

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Let's jump into it.

6
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Picture a server that needs a summary written, but has no model of its own, and no API key.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
That is the problem sampling solves.

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Sampling in MCP is the pattern where a server sends a create message request to the client,

9
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:43.000
the client calls an LLM on the server's behalf, and the result comes back over the MCP channel.

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.000
The piece that makes it work on the client side is the sampling handler,

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.000
a callback you register that receives the request, calls your LLM API, and returns the model's response.

12
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.000
And there's an optional human-in-the-loop gate,

13
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:03.000
an approval step where a user can review the request and accept or reject it before the model is ever called.

14
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Why does sampling exist at all?

15
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.000
The answer is about separating concerns cleanly.

16
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.000
A well-designed MCP server is stateless and focused on its domain,

17
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
covering file operations, database queries, and web search.

18
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
It shouldn't need to hold its own LLM API credentials or worry about which model to use.

19
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
The client already has that relationship.

20
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
It has credentials, it knows the user's preferred model,

21
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.000
and it enforces policy about what data can go to the model.

22
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Sampling reuses all of that existing infrastructure.

23
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Instead of every server independently managing model access,

24
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
which multiplies credential sprawl and policy gaps, sampling centralizes that control.

25
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.000
The client becomes the single control point for all model calls in the system.

26
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.000
That's cleaner architecturally and much easier to audit.

27
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Let's walk through the sampling flow step-by-step so it's clear what happens at each stage.

28
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.000
First, the server decides it needs LLM assistance

29
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.000
and sends a create message request to the client over the standard MCP channel.

30
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.000
That request includes the messages it wants processed,

31
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.000
an optional system prompt, and any model preference hints.

32
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Second, the client receives the request.

33
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:29.000
If human in the loop is configured, it pauses and shows the user the pending request for approval.

34
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Third, the client calls the API once approved, or immediately if no approval gate is set.

35
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Fourth, the model's response comes back to the client,

36
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.000
which packages it as a standard MCP result and returns it to the server.

37
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.000
The server then uses that result just like any other tool response.

38
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.000
One of the most important things to understand about sampling

39
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.000
is the distinction between what the server controls and what the client controls.

40
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.000
The server owns the message content.

41
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.000
It decides what question to ask, what context to include, and what system prompt to use.

42
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.000
It can also express model preferences,

43
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.000
but the client owns the actual model selection, the credentials, and the data policies.

44
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.000
This division is intentional.

45
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.000
It means a server operator can't unilaterally choose an expensive model

46
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.000
or route-sensitive data to an unapproved endpoint.

47
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.000
The client owner, meaning the person deploying the agent, always has the final say.

48
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.000
That separation is what makes sampling safe to enable in production systems.

49
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Implementing a sampling handler in Python is straightforward with the MCPSDK.

50
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.000
You define an async function that accepts a createMessageRequest object,

51
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.000
which contains the messages list, the optional system prompt, and the model preferences.

52
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Inside that function, you call your LLM API.

53
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.000
In most cases, that means the Anthropic API.

54
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.000
Using the message content from the sampling request,

55
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:01.000
you then wrap the model's text response in a createMessageResult and return it.

56
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.000
You register this function on the client before connecting to any servers,

57
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.000
and the SDK automatically routes incoming createMessageRequests to it.

58
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.000
The handler is the only code you need to write.

59
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:18.000
The SDK handles all the serialization and protocol routing around it.

60
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.000
To make the difference concrete, compare sampling to giving a server its own API key.

61
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.000
With direct access, the server calls the LLM independently using its own credentials.

62
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:34.000
You lose visibility into those calls, you can't enforce model selection policy,

63
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.000
and credentials multiply across every server you deploy.

64
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.000
With sampling, all of that disappears.

65
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.000
The server never touches an API key.

66
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.000
Every inference request flows through the client,

67
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.000
which means every call is visible, logged, and subject to the same policies.

68
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.000
If you want to swap models, you change it in one place.

69
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.000
If you want to audit what's being sent to the LLM, you have a single choke point.

70
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.000
Sampling is simply the more secure and maintainable design for any non-trivial deployment.

71
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:11.000
When a server sends a createMessageRequest, it can include model preferences.

72
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.000
These aren't hard requirements, they're suggestions.

73
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.000
There are three priority values, each a number from 0 to 1.

74
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Cost priority says how much minimizing spend matters.

75
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.000
Speed priority says how much low latency matters.

76
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.000
Intelligence priority says how capable the model needs to be.

77
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.000
The server can also send hints, which are model name substrings

78
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.000
The client matches flexibly and evaluates in order.

79
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.000
The key constraint is that the client is always free to honor, substitute, or ignore all of this.

80
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.000
If the user's policy says only one model is approved,

81
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.000
that's what gets used regardless of server preferences.

82
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.000
Preferences let servers communicate needs without demanding control,

83
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.000
which keeps the client firmly in charge.

84
00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.000
Human-in-the-loop sampling is a practical safety feature,

85
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.000
especially during development or when servers are requesting access to sensitive content.

86
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.000
When it's enabled, the client doesn't immediately forward the sampling request to the LLM.

87
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:16.000
Instead, it surfaces the full request so the user can review and approve it,

88
00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.000
including the messages the server wants to send to the model.

89
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.000
The user reviews it and either approves, edits, or rejects it.

90
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.000
Only approved requests move forward.

91
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:32.000
This means a misconfigured or malicious server can't silently pump sensitive data

92
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.000
into a model call without the user noticing.

93
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.000
For production systems handling regulated data,

94
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.000
human-in-the-loop can also serve as a compliance checkpoint,

95
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.000
giving you an auditable record of every approved model interaction.

96
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:53.000
It's easy to conflate a server having intelligence with a server having model access.

97
00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.000
Sampling separates them.

98
00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:00.000
The server asks for an inference, and the client is the one who picks the model,

99
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.000
spins the tokens, and decides whether a human sees it first.

100
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:10.000
That's why you can hand a server real reasoning power and still have exactly one piece to audit.

101
00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.000
That's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

